+### Pictures and parallel requests (`"parallel"`, `--batch`)
+
+With several batch slots the engine keeps one M-RoPE position table per GPU, so by default an image request runs
+**alone**: it waits until every slot has finished, runs on the solo path, and new requests wait behind it
+(`STRATA_IMAGE_WAIT_S`, default 120 s, then 503). A client that resends a picture in every turn of a long
+conversation (an agent session that started with screenshots) therefore makes every turn wait for the others and
+every other client wait for it.
+
+- `STRATA_MROPE_SLOTS=1` (engine env, opt-in): the table holds one region per slot (17 regions of 262,208 rows,
+  51.0 MiB per GPU at 262K context and 16 slots), each slot reads its own, and image and text requests share the
+  batch (`INFO ... mrope_slots=1`; the server then admits image requests into slots like text ones). Without it the
+  engine behaves as before.
+- `STRATA_IMAGE_HISTORY` (server env, opt-in; unset: every picture stays): `tools` turns pictures a tool returned
+  in turns the model has already answered into the note `[image omitted: a picture from an earlier turn]`; `<N>`
+  also turns a picture in a user message right after tool results, and every picture a user sent once N assistant
+  turns have answered it, into that note. The model no longer sees those pictures. The prompt changes once, where
+  the first such picture was, so that turn reads the rest of the conversation again; the pictures of the turn being
+  answered always stay.
+
 ### Sending a picture
 
 **Terminal chat:** type `/image <path to a picture>`, press Enter, then type your question.
@@ -2038,6 +2057,31 @@ Unloading and shutdown close the native engine's stdin after sending `QUIT`, all
 stdin reader to see EOF. Cleanup waits for process exit before releasing handles; if forced shutdown still
 times out, the server keeps ownership and reports an error rather than claiming the model was unloaded.
 
+### Server load for clients (`GET /v1/load`)
+
+`GET /v1/load` (also `GET /load`; same API key rule as `/v1/status`) says how busy the server is, so a client can
+hold back work that is not urgent. `POST /v1/load` and `POST /load` still load the model (above).
+
+```json
+{"load_pct": 75, "level": "busy", "would_wait": false, "image_alone": false, "loaded": true,
+ "slots": {"total": 16, "busy": 12, "decoding": 10, "reading_prompt": 2, "free": 4, "waiting": 0},
+ "tok_s": {"now": 250.0, "peak": 660.0, "share_pct": 38}, "server_load": 12,
+ "advice": "defer non-urgent work", "time": 1791900000.0}
+```
+
+- `load_pct` is the larger of two shares: the token rate of the last 3 s against the 100 % mark, and the slots in use
+  against all slots. `level`: `idle` below 25 %, `low` below 50 %, `busy` below 80 %, `saturated` from there.
+- `slots.waiting` counts every request that waits once: for the control lines (one prompt read at a time), for a
+  slot, behind an image request, or before it is registered. `would_wait`: a new request would wait now.
+- `image_alone`: an image request has the engine alone or waits for the slots to empty (without the per-slot M-RoPE
+  regions, see Images); nothing new is admitted meanwhile, so the slots count as all taken.
+- The 100 % mark (`tok_s.peak`) is the highest token rate **held for 10 s while at least half the slots decoded** (one
+  request's speed is not the server's capacity, and a one-second figure can be a burst). It is kept next to the run
+  config (`<config>.load-peak.json`, or the file `STRATA_LOAD_PEAK_FILE` names) with the engine settings it was
+  measured with; a peak measured with another engine or config is not taken. `STRATA_LOAD_REF_TOK_S=<tok/s>` sets the
+  mark by hand. Until one is known, `tok_s.peak` is `null` and only the slots count.
+- `server_load` is vLLM's `GET /load` figure: the requests in flight, queued ones included.
+
